# Architecture

## Rules

- Use feature-first packages as the default.
- Do not use layer packages named `controller`, `service`, `repository`, or `exception`.
- Place every new production class under its owning feature root package.
- Use `br.com.<organization>.<module-name>` as the root Java package; derive the module segment from `artifactId`.
- Name feature sub-packages with the bounded-context term.
- Do not repeat the module segment as the immediate child package (invalid: `com.org.module.module`).
- Keep feature-scoped `*Impl`, `@Component`/`@Configuration`/`@ConfigurationProperties`, controllers, and domain models package-private.
- Expose only public interfaces, DTOs (`*Request`, `*Response`), and explicit cross-feature contracts from a feature package.
- Place types with no single feature owner in `shared`, organized by concept (no `shared/service`).
- Treat a type as `shared` only when two or more distinct features depend on it.
- Keep feature-specific exceptions and error codes in the owning feature package.

## Package layout

```text
# GOOD — feature package (artifactId: holidays)
br.com.lsampaioweb.holidays.holiday/
  HolidayController.java
  HolidayService.java
  HolidayRepository.java
  HolidayRepositoryImpl.java
  HolidayModel.java
  HolidayRequest.java
  HolidayResponse.java
br.com.lsampaioweb.holidays.shared/
  ApiResponse.java

# BAD — layer packages and demo roots
com.example.demo.controller.HolidayController.java
com.example.demo.service.HolidayService.java
com.example.demo.repository.HolidayRepository.java
br.com.org.holidays.holidays.HolidayController.java
```

## Safety Guards

- Never use JPA, Jakarta Persistence, Hibernate, `@Entity`, or any ORM in any application layer.
